ng multi-year lows against a dollar  and the last time the pound was sizably  weaker than this was way back in 1985.  so clearly while the us dollar is  getting crushed in terms of buying power  it's actually gaining dominance on the  world currencies so what in the world is  going on with a dollar  starting with the biggest culprit we  have supply and demand though we often  like to think of currencies as a stable  medium of exchange during regular times  currencies are actually subject to all  of the same supply and demand  characteristics that affect something  like the stock market currencies simply  have extremely low volatility during  regular times however with the  groundbreaking events like printing  trillions of dollars followed up by  suddenly raising interest rates it's not  surprising that even currencies are  experiencing abnormally high volatility  also something to note is that it's not  just the us who has been engaging in  such activities either most of the world  central banks have engaged in very  similar monetary policies take the  european central bank or the ecb for  example since the pandemic started it  they have also pumped trillions of euros  into the market now this isn't quite as  high as the us in terms of dollars but  the thing to note is that relative to  economic metrics the money printing of  other central banks are just as high if  not higher for example the total gdp of  all the european union countries that  use the euro is 12.23 trillion dollars  while this is an extremely respectable  figure it's only about 60 of the united  states 20.94 trillion gdp now this isn't  an exact science by any means but this  leads us to believe that the ecba cannot  print more than 60 of the dollars  printed by the us if they want to  maintain the exchange rate between the  two currencies this same principle  applies to smaller economies as well the  uk for example has a gdp of 2.7 trillion  dollars which is a little more than an  eighth of the us given that the us has  increased their money supply by about  6.3 trillion dollars this means that the  uk could increase their money supply by  about 800 billion dollars however the uk  has surpassed this threshold by quite a  bit so far the uk has printed 895  billion pounds if we use the average  pound to dollar conversion rate in 2021  of a  1.3757 we'll see that this money  printing correlates to 1.231 trillion  dollars which is over 50 higher than  what they should have theoretically  printed if you look into the money  printing of some other leading nations  you'll see that they have fallen into  the same trap of printing more money  than the us on a relative basis on top  of this many of these countries aren't  pulling back nearly as aggressively as  the us currently is and for obvious  reasons this has led to the us dollar  performing quite well in relation to  these foreign currencies  [Music]  aside from fundamental supply and demand  factors external fears have also  influenced the dollar's recent parabolic  move one of the top concerns for most  investors right now is how hawkish the  fed will be over the next couple of  months currently there are rumors  floating around that the fed will hike  rates by 75 basis points not once but  twice both in june and july whether this  ends up happening or not the stock  market has already preemptively  corrected for this some would even argue  that it has over corrected for this  regardless of whether the market  sell-off is justified or not though in  the end significant amounts of capital  have left the market by now i'm sure  most of you guys have seen my videos on  how several companies have been shedding  hundreds of billions of dollars in  market cap and while a lot of this value  just evaporates away the primary place  the remaining capital is going is cash  which is significantly driving up the  demand for us dollars something else to  note is that it's only the stock market  that has acted preemptively t